{"id": "bio-0001", "question_text": "Which organelle is known as the powerhouse of the cell?", "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Mitochondria", "is_correct": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "Ribosome", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "C", "text": "Golgi apparatus", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "D", "text": "Lysosome", "is_correct": false}], "answer_text": "Mitochondria is known as the powerhouse of the cell because it generates ATP through oxidative phosphorylation, the cell's main energy currency.", "concept_tag": "Cell Biology", "subconcept_tag": "Cell Organelles", "misconception_tags": {"B": "confuses-protein-synthesis-site-with-energy-site", "C": "confuses-packaging-organelle-with-energy-site", "D": "confuses-digestive-organelle-with-energy-site"}, "difficulty": "easy", "source": "NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 8", "verified_by": "internal-v0-seed", "exam_tier": "NEET", "language": "en"}
{"id": "bio-0002", "question_text": "What is the site of light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle) in a chloroplast?", "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Stroma", "is_correct": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "Thylakoid membrane", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "C", "text": "Outer membrane", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "D", "text": "Intermembrane space", "is_correct": false}], "answer_text": "The stroma is the site of the Calvin cycle (light-independent reactions), where CO2 is fixed into glucose using the ATP and NADPH produced during the light reactions.", "concept_tag": "Plant Physiology", "subconcept_tag": "Photosynthesis", "misconception_tags": {"B": "confuses-light-reaction-site-with-dark-reaction-site", "C": "confuses-organelle-boundary-with-reaction-site", "D": "confuses-compartment-with-reaction-site"}, "difficulty": "medium", "source": "NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13", "verified_by": "internal-v0-seed", "exam_tier": "NEET", "language": "en"}
{"id": "bio-0003", "question_text": "In a dihybrid cross between two heterozygous pea plants (RrYy x RrYy), what is the phenotypic ratio of the offspring?", "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "9:3:3:1", "is_correct": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "3:1", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "C", "text": "1:1:1:1", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "D", "text": "12:3:1", "is_correct": false}], "answer_text": "The phenotypic ratio is 9:3:3:1 (9 round-yellow : 3 round-green : 3 wrinkled-yellow : 1 wrinkled-green), following Mendel's law of independent assortment.", "concept_tag": "Genetics and Evolution", "subconcept_tag": "Mendelian Inheritance", "misconception_tags": {"B": "confuses-monohybrid-ratio-with-dihybrid-ratio", "C": "assumes-independent-assortment-yields-equal-ratio", "D": "confuses-dihybrid-ratio-with-epistasis-ratio"}, "difficulty": "medium", "source": "NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 5", "verified_by": "internal-v0-seed", "exam_tier": "NEET", "language": "en"}
{"id": "bio-0004", "question_text": "Which part of the human nephron is primarily responsible for the reabsorption of glucose and amino acids?", "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Proximal convoluted tubule", "is_correct": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "Loop of Henle", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "C", "text": "Distal convoluted tubule", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "D", "text": "Collecting duct", "is_correct": false}], "answer_text": "The proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) is primarily responsible for reabsorbing glucose, amino acids, and most ions back into the blood.", "concept_tag": "Human Physiology", "subconcept_tag": "Excretory System", "misconception_tags": {"B": "confuses-water-reabsorption-site-with-nutrient-reabsorption-site", "C": "confuses-secretion-site-with-reabsorption-site", "D": "confuses-final-concentration-site-with-reabsorption-site"}, "difficulty": "medium", "source": "NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 19", "verified_by": "internal-v0-seed", "exam_tier": "NEET", "language": "en"}
{"id": "bio-0005", "question_text": "What term describes the total amount of organic matter or energy produced by producers per unit area over a period of time?", "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Gross Primary Productivity", "is_correct": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "Net Primary Productivity", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "C", "text": "Secondary Productivity", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "D", "text": "Standing Crop", "is_correct": false}], "answer_text": "Gross Primary Productivity (GPP) is the total rate of energy or organic matter production by producers through photosynthesis, before accounting for respiratory losses.", "concept_tag": "Ecology", "subconcept_tag": "Ecosystem Productivity", "misconception_tags": {"B": "confuses-gross-with-net-productivity", "C": "confuses-producer-level-with-consumer-level-productivity", "D": "confuses-productivity-rate-with-biomass-quantity"}, "difficulty": "hard", "source": "NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 14", "verified_by": "internal-v0-seed", "exam_tier": "NEET", "language": "en"}
{"id": "bio-0006", "question_text": "Which part of a neuron receives incoming signals from other neurons via synapses and conducts them towards the cell body?", "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Dendrites", "is_correct": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "Axon", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "C", "text": "Cell body (soma)", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "D", "text": "Axon terminal / synaptic knob", "is_correct": false}], "answer_text": "Dendrites are short, branched processes that receive stimuli from neighbouring neurons and conduct impulses towards the cell body.", "concept_tag": "Human Physiology", "subconcept_tag": "Neural Control and Coordination", "misconception_tags": {"B": "confuses-input-structure-with-output-structure", "C": "confuses-reception-site-with-integration-site", "D": "confuses-input-structure-with-transmission-terminal"}, "difficulty": "easy", "source": "NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 21", "verified_by": "pending", "exam_tier": "NEET", "language": "en"}
{"id": "bio-0007", "question_text": "Which hormone acts on Sertoli cells in the seminiferous tubules to stimulate spermatogenesis?", "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "FSH (Follicle Stimulating Hormone)", "is_correct": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "LH (Luteinizing Hormone)", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "C", "text": "Testosterone", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "D", "text": "Estrogen", "is_correct": false}], "answer_text": "FSH acts on Sertoli cells to stimulate spermatogenesis, while LH acts on Leydig cells to stimulate secretion of testosterone.", "concept_tag": "Human Reproduction", "subconcept_tag": "Spermatogenesis", "misconception_tags": {"B": "confuses-FSH-Sertoli-axis-with-LH-Leydig-axis", "C": "confuses-stimulating-hormone-with-downstream-product", "D": "confuses-male-hormonal-regulation-with-female-specific-hormone"}, "difficulty": "medium", "source": "NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 3", "verified_by": "pending", "exam_tier": "NEET", "language": "en"}
{"id": "bio-0008", "question_text": "In a chloroplast, where do the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis occur?", "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Thylakoid membrane", "is_correct": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "Stroma", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "C", "text": "Outer membrane", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "D", "text": "Intermembrane space", "is_correct": false}], "answer_text": "The light-dependent reactions occur on the thylakoid membrane, where photosystems I and II capture light energy to generate ATP and NADPH.", "concept_tag": "Plant Physiology", "subconcept_tag": "Photosynthesis", "misconception_tags": {"B": "confuses-light-reaction-site-with-dark-reaction-site", "C": "confuses-organelle-boundary-with-reaction-site", "D": "confuses-compartment-with-reaction-site"}, "difficulty": "medium", "source": "NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13", "verified_by": "pending", "exam_tier": "NEET", "language": "en"}
{"id": "bio-0009", "question_text": "Restriction endonucleases used in recombinant DNA technology recognize and cut DNA at specific sequences known as:", "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Palindromic sequences", "is_correct": true}, {"id": "B", "text": "Promoter sequences", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "C", "text": "Operator sequences", "is_correct": false}, {"id": "D", "text": "Termination sequences", "is_correct": false}], "answer_text": "Restriction endonucleases recognize specific palindromic base sequences in DNA and cut both strands at those sites, producing fragments used in recombinant DNA technology.", "concept_tag": "Biotechnology", "subconcept_tag": "Recombinant DNA Technology", "misconception_tags": {"B": "confuses-restriction-site-with-gene-expression-regulatory-sequence", "C": "confuses-restriction-site-with-operon-regulatory-sequence", "D": "confuses-restriction-site-with-transcription-termination-sequence"}, "difficulty": "medium", "source": "NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 11", "verified_by": "pending", "exam_tier": "NEET", "language": "en"}
